## Appendix — reference fix patterns

{Copy these in verbatim, so the report is self-contained even if the reader never sees
this skill file:}

**A — Missing accessible name (button/link/control).** Add visible text where possible;
otherwise `aria-label` describing the action ("Close dialog", not "Icon button"). Never
rely on `title` alone — it's not reliably exposed by screen readers and isn't visible on
touch devices.

**B — Image alt text.** Meaningful images: describe the content/function in `alt`, not
the filename. Purely decorative images: `alt=""` (empty, not omitted) so screen readers
skip them silently. An image that's the *only* content of a link/button: its `alt` must
convey the link's destination/action, not just describe the picture.

**C — Form label association.** Use `<label for="id">` or wrap the control in a
`<label>`. If a visible label isn't in the design, use `aria-label` — never rely on
`placeholder` as the only name, since it vanishes on input and isn't a reliable
accessible-name source across screen readers. If the field already has label-shaped
text sitting next to it visually, see pattern I below — the fix is to wire up what's
already there, not add new copy. If the concern is the opposite — a real accessible
name exists but nothing is visible on screen — see pattern J.

**D — Heading hierarchy.** Headings should step down one level at a time
(`h1`→`h2`→`h3`), reflecting document structure, not visual size — use CSS for size,
heading level for structure.

**E — Landmark regions.** Wrap primary content in a single `<main>`; use `<nav>`,
`<header>`, `<footer>` for their semantic roles rather than generic `<div>`s, so screen
reader users can jump between regions instead of tabbing through everything linearly.

**F — Focus indicator.** Never ship `outline: none` without a replacement focus style.
A `:focus-visible` box-shadow or outline with sufficient contrast against both light and
dark surrounding content satisfies this without also showing on mouse clicks if that's
the desired UX.

**G — Color contrast.** Darken text or lighten background until the ratio clears 4.5:1
(normal text) or 3:1 (large/bold text ≥24px or ≥18.66px+bold). Check both the default
and any hover/disabled/placeholder states — these frequently regress contrast even when
the base state passes.

**H — Skip link.** First focusable element on the page, visually hidden until focused,
`href="#main-content"` pointing at an element with a matching `id` (and `tabindex="-1"`
on that target if it isn't natively focusable) so activating the link actually moves
focus, not just scroll position.

**I — Visual-only or orphaned label.** The label text already exists in the design —
the bug is purely the missing/broken association, so don't write new copy. If the
"label" is a `<span>`/`<div>`/table cell sitting next to the field with no relationship
to it: convert it to a real `<label for="id">` (give the control an `id` if it doesn't
have one) or move the existing text inside a wrapping `<label>`. If a `<label>` already
exists but its `for` points at an id that doesn't match anything (typo, or stale after a
rename/refactor): fix the `for` value to the control's actual `id` — don't add a second
label, since two labels racing for the same control is its own failure mode. Re-verify
by checking the control's computed accessible name equals the visible text, not just
that a `for`/`id` pair exists syntactically.

**J — Missing visible label (accessible name exists, nothing shows on screen).** If
relying on `aria-label` alone: add real visible text and switch to `<label for="id">`
(or wrap the control), reserving `aria-label` for cases where a visible label is
deliberately not part of the design (icon-only search/filter fields) — and in that case,
pair it with a persistent visual affordance (icon, static prefix) rather than an empty
box, since a `placeholder` alone still disappears on input. If a real `<label>` exists
but is visually hidden via an `sr-only`-style utility class: keep it for screen readers,
but confirm a sighted user still has *some* persistent visual cue for the field's
purpose — if not, either make the label visible or add one. Don't simply delete the
hidden label to "fix" this — that regresses screen reader support to solve a sighted-user
problem; the fix is to add a visible cue alongside what's already there for AT users. If
a visible text run already sits right next to the field (a "Visible label disconnected
from accessible name" finding) but the accessible name comes from a separate parallel
`aria-label`: replace the `aria-label` with `aria-labelledby` pointing at that existing
visible element's `id` (adding one if it doesn't have one) rather than keeping two
independent strings that can drift out of sync — one source of truth for both audiences.
```

After writing the file, tell the user in chat: the output path, the summary counts, the
single most severe finding, and whether the page has custom dropdowns/menus that
warrant a follow-up `/keyboard-dropdown-audit` run — not the full findings list again.
The file is where the detail lives.
